// A8 × A9 — a tuple whose ELEMENTS are slices of a type parameter, which is the one shape that got
// past `burxt layout`'s concreteness test.
//
// **This fixture exists because the bug was invisible to every test that already existed.** The
// layout computation was never wrong: `([T], [T])` was sized 48/24/24, both compilers ran the
// program identically, and every `.stdout` matched. What differed was the NAME in the report —
// stage-0 said `([Int], [Int])` and stage-1 said `([T], [T])` — and that only broke
// `the_two_compilers_report_the_same_layout`, which is one test out of the whole suite.
//
// ---- why nothing caught it for six versions ---------------------------------------------
//
// `layout_is_concrete` refused a type parameter by asking `argument.kind == 50`. A bare `T` IS kind
// 50, so `(T, T)` was correctly refused and the scalar case worked from the day A8 landed. A slice
// of a parameter is kind 47 holding a 50, and the test stopped at the 47 — so `([T], [T])` was
// called concrete and reported as a type no value has.
//
// **A8 worked and A9 worked; the defect lived only where a slice sat between them.** Nothing found
// it until `array_partition<T>(xs, p) -> ([T], [T])` was written in `lib/array.bx`, at which point
// it broke the layout agreement for every fixture that used that module — including two written
// long before tuples existed.
//
// ---- and the second half, which is a different gap --------------------------------------
//
// `Bag` below has a method returning `(K, V)` that **nothing calls**. Stage-0 specialises every
// method of every instantiation, so it files a `(String, Int)` class; stage-1 does not
// monomorphise, so it has to find that tuple by walking the instantiation's methods itself. That
// is a separate pass in `layout.bx` and it is what `lib/map.bx`'s `entries() -> [(K, V)]` needs —
// without it, `map_library.bx` and `set_library.bx` each differed by three rows.
//
// `Set` here is the nested case in miniature: the instantiation whose method matters
// (`Bag<Int, Bool>`) is never written down — it exists only because `Holder<Int>` has a field of
// that type. A pass that scanned the source would miss it, which is why that one is a worklist.
//
// The program's OUTPUT is almost beside the point; `burxt layout` is what this pins. It is a
// `tests/pass` fixture rather than a layout-only one because the harness compares the two
// compilers' layout reports over every pass fixture, so landing it here is what makes it checked.

class Bag<K, V> { k: K, v: V }

// Never called. Stage-0 still builds `(String, Int)` and `(Int, Bool)` for the instantiations below.
function (self: Bag<K, V>) both() -> (K, V) {
    return (self.k, self.v);
}

// The nested case: `Bag<Int, Bool>` is never spelled out anywhere.
class Holder<T> { inner: Bag<T, Bool> }

// The shape that started it: a tuple of two SLICES of a type parameter, returned from a free
// generic function. Annotated at the call site below, because stage-1 cannot report a tuple that
// only exists inside a specialisation it never made.
function halve<T>(xs: [T]) -> ([T], [T]) {
    let mutable front: [T] = [];
    let mutable back: [T] = [];
    let half: Int = divide_floor(len(xs), 2);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if i < half {
            push(front, xs[i]);
        } else {
            push(back, xs[i]);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return (front, back);
}

region main {
    let numbers: [Int] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    let split: ([Int], [Int]) = halve(numbers);
    let front: [Int] = split.0;
    let back: [Int] = split.1;
    print(len(front));
    print(len(back));
    print(front[0]);
    print(back[0]);

    let labelled: Bag<String, Int> = Bag { k: "a", v: 1 };
    print(labelled.k);

    let held: Holder<Int> = Holder { inner: Bag { k: 7, v: true } };
    print(held.inner.k);
}
